detailedExplanation.whatItIs:
A closure is created whenever a function is defined inside another function and uses its variables. Example:
function counter() {
  let count = 0;
  return function() { return ++count; };
}
const next = counter();
next(); // 1
next(); // 2
The inner function 'closes over' count.

this is a special keyword referring to the execution context of a function. Examples:
const obj = {
  name: 'Ana',
  greet() { return this.name; }
};
obj.greet();   // 'Ana', this is obj
const g = obj.greet;
g();           // undefined or error, this is lost
Its value shifts based on the call.

Three methods available on every function. Examples:
function greet(greeting) { return greeting + ', ' + this.name; }
const user = { name: 'Ana' };
greet.call(user, 'Hi');        // 'Hi, Ana'
greet.apply(user, ['Hi']);     // 'Hi, Ana' (array args)
const bound = greet.bind(user);
bound('Hi');                   // 'Hi, Ana' (called later)

A prototype is an object that another object inherits from. Example:
const animal = { eats: true };
const dog = Object.create(animal);
dog.barks = true;
dog.eats;   // true, inherited from animal
Here dog's prototype is animal, so dog can access eats even though it does not own it.

Scope defines variable visibility. Example:
const a = 1;            // global
function outer() {
  const b = 2;          // outer scope
  function inner() {
    const c = 3;        // inner scope
    return a + b + c;   // can see all three
  }
  return inner();
}
inner can reach b and a by following the chain.

Same syntax, opposite jobs. Examples:
// rest: collects arguments
function sum(...nums) { return nums.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0); }
sum(1, 2, 3);   // 6

// spread: expands an array
const arr = [1, 2, 3];
Math.max(...arr);  // 3
Rest is in the definition; spread is in the call or literal.

A Promise is a placeholder for a future value. Example:
const p = fetch('/data')
  .then(response => response.json())
  .then(data => console.log(data))
  .catch(error => console.error(error));
The promise resolves with data later, and handlers run then.

async/await wraps promises in linear-looking code. Example:
async function loadUser() {
  try {
    const res = await fetch('/user');
    const data = await res.json();
    return data;
  } catch (err) {
    console.error(err);
  }
}
The awaits read top to bottom like normal code.

The event loop coordinates running your code with handling async results. Conceptually:
console.log('A');
setTimeout(() => console.log('B'), 0);
console.log('C');
// Output: A, C, B
B runs only after the synchronous code finishes, thanks to the event loop.

Error handling catches and responds to failures. Example:
try {
  const data = JSON.parse(input);
} catch (err) {
  console.error('Invalid JSON:', err.message);
} finally {
  cleanup();
}
throw new Error('Something failed'); // create an error

An IIFE runs as soon as it is defined. Example:
(function() {
  const secret = 42;
  console.log(secret);
})();
// or arrow form
(() => {
  // private scope here
})();
The variables inside are not accessible outside.

Currying breaks a multi-argument function into chained single-argument calls. Example:
const add = a => b => c => a + b + c;
add(1)(2)(3);   // 6
const addTen = add(10);
addTen(5)(2);   // 17
Each call returns a function expecting the next argument.

Two rate-limiting strategies for event handlers. Conceptually:
// debounce: run 300ms after the LAST keystroke
searchInput.addEventListener('input', debounce(search, 300));
// throttle: run at most once every 200ms while scrolling
window.addEventListener('scroll', throttle(onScroll, 200));
Debounce collapses bursts; throttle caps frequency.

Two levels of copying. Example:
const original = { name: 'Ana', address: { city: 'NYC' } };
const shallow = { ...original };
shallow.address.city = 'LA';      // also changes original!
const deep = structuredClone(original);
deep.address.city = 'LA';         // original unaffected

Map is a built-in keyed collection. Example:
const m = new Map();
m.set('name', 'Ana');
m.set(42, 'number key');
m.set(objKey, 'object key');
m.get('name');   // 'Ana'
m.size;          // 3
Objects, by contrast, use obj.key with string keys only.

A Set holds unique values of any type. Example:
const s = new Set([1, 2, 2, 3]);
s.size;           // 3, duplicate 2 ignored
s.has(2);         // true
s.add(4);
s.delete(1);
const unique = [...new Set([1,1,2,3])]; // [1,2,3]
Duplicates are automatically removed.

Higher-order functions operate on other functions. Examples:
// takes a function
[1, 2, 3].map(n => n * 2);
// returns a function
function multiplier(factor) {
  return n => n * factor;
}
const double = multiplier(2);
double(5); // 10

Two execution models. Example:
console.log('1');
console.log('2');           // synchronous, in order

console.log('A');
setTimeout(() => console.log('B'), 1000);
console.log('C');           // logs A, C, then B later
The async setTimeout does not block.
